From 9777be92cc00190c495aa12876ce1b069a4306f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 22:12:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] microblaze: expand strlen inline with pcmpbf

With -mxl-pattern-compare, MicroBlaze has pcmpbf, which returns 1 to 4
for the first byte of rA, counting from the most significant, that
equals the same byte of rB, and 0 if none does.  Against r0 it finds a
zero byte in one instruction.  GCC used pattern compare only for
cstoresi4 (pcmpeq and pcmpne), and strlen was always a call.

Add a strlensi expander.  For a string known to be word-aligned it
loads a word at a time until pcmpbf finds a zero byte, a loop of four
instructions including the delay slot.  Without a known alignment, at
-Os, or without -mxl-pattern-compare it fails and strlen stays a call.
This follows the rs6000 strlensi expander, which does the same with
dlmzb, and riscv_expand_strlen_scalar, which uses orc.b; both require
the alignment, because an aligned word load cannot fault where a byte
load of the string would not.

pcmpbf numbers the bytes in register order, so on a little-endian core
the first byte in memory is byte 4.  There the loop still finds the
word.  With -mxl-reorder the word is reversed with swapb and searched
again; otherwise the zero byte is found with byte loads.

Under QEMU with -icount, strlen of a word-aligned 255-byte string takes
263 instructions inline on big-endian, against 601 calling newlib's
microblaze strlen.c and 346 calling it built with -mxl-pattern-compare;
an empty string takes 11, against 25 and 19.

Tested on microblaze-elf (big-endian) with gcc.target/microblaze and
gcc.c-torture/execute under QEMU.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

